= ECON 0100 | Homework B3 | Equilibrium | Solutions

_Solution guide for the teaching team. Answers and working are in the red blocks; everything else is what students see. Students answer on paper and submit selections on Gradescope, where the homework is completion-graded. The working follows the archived Fall 2022 butterbeer guide: set the two prices equal for equilibrium, then areas as height times base times a half._

== Butterbeer

Preferences for butter beer can be represented by the following demand curve:

#mitex(`
P_d = 100 - \frac{1}{2} Q_d
`)

The supply curve for butter beer can be represented by the following equation:

#mitex(`
P = 20 + \frac{1}{2} Q
`)

Prices are in galleons and quantity is in bottles.

== Q1 | Equilibrium

a) Calculate equilibrium price: \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

b) Find equilibrium quantity: \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

c) Calculate consumer surplus: \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

d) Calculate producer surplus: \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  *a)* $60$ galleons. *b)* $80$ bottles. *c)* $1600$ galleons. *d)* $1600$ galleons.

  #grid(
    columns: (1fr, 1fr),
    gutter: 1em,
    [
      At equilibrium the same price and quantity sit on both curves, $Q_d = Q_s$, so set the two prices equal, solve for $Q$, and read the price off either curve:

      #mitex(`
      100 - \tfrac{1}{2} Q = 20 + \tfrac{1}{2} Q \rightarrow 80 = Q \rightarrow Q^* = 80
      `)

      #mitex(`
      P^* = 20 + \tfrac{1}{2} \cdot 80 = 60 \quad (= 100 - \tfrac{1}{2} \cdot 80)
      `)

      Consumer surplus is the triangle below demand and above $P^*$; producer surplus is the triangle above supply and below $P^*$, both out to $Q^*$:

      #mitex(`
      \text{CS} = h \cdot b \cdot \tfrac{1}{2} = (100 - 60) \cdot 80 \cdot \tfrac{1}{2} = 1600
      `)

      #mitex(`
      \text{PS} = h \cdot b \cdot \tfrac{1}{2} = (60 - 20) \cdot 80 \cdot \tfrac{1}{2} = 1600
      `)

      The two triangles are equal because the curves have the same slope, so $P^*$ sits midway between the intercepts.
    ],
    [
      #image("data:image/svg+xml;base64,", width: 100%)
    ],
  )
]

== Q2 | A Price Below Equilibrium

Butter beer again. Suppose the price is $50$ galleons.

a) What is the quantity demanded? \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

b) What is the quantity supplied? \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

c) Is this a shortage or an excess, and how large? \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

d) Which way will the price move? \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  *a)* $100$ bottles. *b)* $60$ bottles. *c)* A shortage of $40$ bottles. *d)* Up.

  Read each curve at the going price.

  *a)* $50 = 100 - \frac{1}{2} Q_d \rightarrow \frac{1}{2} Q_d = 50 \rightarrow Q_d = 100$

  *b)* $50 = 20 + \frac{1}{2} Q_s \rightarrow 30 = \frac{1}{2} Q_s \rightarrow Q_s = 60$

  *c)* Buyers want $100$ and sellers offer $60$: $Q_d > Q_s$, a shortage of $100 - 60 = 40$ bottles. Only $60$ bottles change hands, the smaller of the two.

  *d)* Buyers who cannot find a bottle offer more, and sellers see they can charge more and still sell out, so the price rises toward $60$, where the shortage closes.
]

== Q3 | A Price Above Equilibrium

Suppose instead the price is $70$ galleons.

a) What is the quantity demanded? \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

b) What is the quantity supplied? \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

c) Is this a shortage or an excess, and how large? \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

d) Which way will the price move? \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  *a)* $60$ bottles. *b)* $100$ bottles. *c)* An excess of $40$ bottles. *d)* Down.

  #grid(
    columns: (1fr, 1fr),
    gutter: 1em,
    [
      *a)* $70 = 100 - \frac{1}{2} Q_d \rightarrow \frac{1}{2} Q_d = 30 \rightarrow Q_d = 60$

      *b)* $70 = 20 + \frac{1}{2} Q_s \rightarrow 50 = \frac{1}{2} Q_s \rightarrow Q_s = 100$

      *c)* Sellers offer $100$ and buyers take $60$: $Q_s > Q_d$, an excess of $100 - 60 = 40$ bottles; again $60$ are exchanged.

      *d)* Sellers stuck with unsold bottles cut the price, and buyers hold out for the lower offers, so the price falls toward $60$.

      The two prices are mirror images: $10$ below equilibrium leaves $40$ bottles short, $10$ above leaves $40$ unsold, and either way the market moves back to $(80, 60)$.
    ],
    [
      #image("data:image/svg+xml;base64,", width: 100%)
    ],
  )
]

